WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:13.271
<v Jamie>Microsoft's up fifteen point five-one percent, and that's not even the headline—turns out the AI trade is splitting the market.

00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:30.025
<v Alex>The tape closed with the S&P at seven thousand four hundred thirty-seven, up one point six-six percent. Dow up one point one-nine percent. Nasdaq took the lead with two point seven-eight percent—the Russell two thousand keeping pace at one point three-seven. Breadth is there. The VIX dropped seventeen point two-eight percent, which means volatility evaporated.

00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:33.316
<v Jamie>So we're just... calm now? After the week we've had?

00:00:33.316 --> 00:00:39.598
<v Alex>You've got the classic risk-on setup: equities rallying, rates climbing, gold catching a bid because nobody's actually confident in anything.

00:00:39.598 --> 00:00:45.582
<v Maya>Gold's the only thing that's honest about this market right now. Everything else is pretending it knows what's happening.

00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:53.959
<v Jamie>And we're watching this whole thing with Microsoft and AMD just running—fifteen point five-one percent and thirteen percent respectively. That's not a market move, that's a stampede.

00:00:53.959 --> 00:01:08.320
<v Alex>Oracle up eight point three-four percent, Broadcom up four point seven-three. Amazon up three point nine. Meanwhile Meta's down seven point nine-five, Adobe's off five point nine, Accenture down five point seven-one. The tape's sorting itself: infrastructure and AI chips are in, consumer-facing software and consulting are out.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:16.098
<v Jamie>So Meta's guidance missed—the market looked at that and said, 'Yeah, we're done.' Eight percent down on a day when basically everything else is up.

00:01:16.098 --> 00:01:24.774
<v Alex>They also missed revenue guidance. That's the thing people aren't screaming about loud enough. Microsoft gets a beat and guidance raise, Meta gets a beat and guidance miss.

00:01:24.774 --> 00:01:31.057
<v Maya>And here's the problem—Meta's spending is still going up. They're burning cash on AI infrastructure while the cash flow's evaporating.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:38.836
<v Jamie>But Azure and Copilot at Microsoft—those are actual revenue drivers right now. Satya's not just talking about AI, he's got paying customers. That's the split.

00:01:38.836 --> 00:01:47.213
<v Alex>The market's drawing a line: infrastructure plays that monetize AI get rewarded, consumer-facing platforms that are still figuring out how to turn AI into cash get punished.

00:01:47.213 --> 00:01:52.000
<v Maya>Enough that people are actually counting. The streak matters because it means the narrative's shifted.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.881
<v Jamie>And that's the thing—Microsoft gets to spend on AI and everyone cheers. Meta spends on AI and everyone asks for the exit sign.

00:01:58.881 --> 00:02:05.463
<v Alex>The UK Supreme Court just gave Palestine Action's co-founder permission to appeal the government's proscription of the group under anti-terrorism legislation.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c87n321w9qlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:02:05.463 --> 00:02:12.344
<v Jamie>That's not a small thing. The government already banned them, and now the court's saying, hold on, let's look at this again.

00:02:12.344 --> 00:02:20.421
<v Maya>Apple just posted iPhone sales up in the latest quarter, and here's the thing—they did it while the entire chip industry is drowning in RAM shortages.

00:02:20.421 --> 00:02:25.208
<v Jamie>That's the inverse of what we usually see. Shortages hurt sales. Apple's defying the script.

00:02:25.208 --> 00:02:29.696
<v Alex>They've got the supply chain relationships and the pricing power to absorb the shortage.

00:02:29.696 --> 00:02:34.782
<v Jamie>Meta's stock got hammered again. Down hard on a day when the rest of tech's flying.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/metas-stock-is-falling-hard-heres-why-the-company-is-in-wall-streets-doghouse-afae2f55?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:02:34.782 --> 00:02:44.057
<v Maya>They beat earnings. They beat revenue. But they guided down on future revenue and they're not showing a path to profitability in the areas where they're burning the most cash.

00:02:44.057 --> 00:02:48.245
<v Alex>The market's not punishing them for missing—it's punishing them for missing while spending more.

00:02:48.245 --> 00:02:54.827
<v Jamie>Tim Cook just said Apple's hybrid AI strategy is a competitive weapon. They're going to charge for artificial intelligence through iCloud.

00:02:54.827 --> 00:02:59.015
<v Maya>That's the same bet that worked for them with music and cloud storage.

00:02:59.015 --> 00:03:07.093
<v Alex>The difference is Apple controls the device. You want Siri AI? You're already in the ecosystem. They don't need to compete on price—they compete on integration.

00:03:07.093 --> 00:03:15.769
<v Maya>Rivian just cut its spending plans and narrowed its losses for this year, which sounds like a win until you realize they're doing it because they're hemorrhaging cash.

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:22.351
<v Jamie>So they're pulling back on capital expenditure—that's the infrastructure spend, the factory buildout. What does that actually mean for production timelines?

00:03:22.351 --> 00:03:26.839
<v Alex>It means they're slowing down the thing that was supposed to justify their valuation.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:35.814
<v Maya>Meta's lost ground while Microsoft prints money on the same bet. The market's decided which company gets to gamble with shareholder cash and which one has to actually win.

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:41.200
<v Alex>One's building the infrastructure the other one needs. The other one's hoping the infrastructure eventually does something.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.388
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
